## kix diff

Compare the build with the live cluster or a directory, read-only

Compares the build against the live cluster's Kix-managed resources (requiring only `get` and `list` permissions), a snapshot from `kix snapshot save`, or another build (`--from <DIR>`, which requires no cluster access). When the currently deployed build is available locally, field changes are classified as either committed changes or live drift.

Because `kix diff` is read-only, it cannot detect write-time effects such as admission webhook mutations or rejections, server-side apply ownership conflicts, or deploy-time refusals. Use `kix plan` to check those with a server-side dry run.

Exits 2 when any differences are found.

Usage: kix diff [OPTIONS] <CLUSTER>

Arguments:
  <CLUSTER>
          Cluster name

Options:
      --from <PATH>
          Compare against a local build or directory instead of the live cluster: a previous build (its `kix.run/built-via` store path, or a result directory containing `kix-packages.json`) or a manifest directory from `kix snapshot save` or `kix export`

      --bundle <FILE>
          Read the target build from a `.kixbundle` file instead of evaluating. Without `--from` or `--from-bundle`, compares against an empty baseline without contacting the cluster

      --from-bundle <FILE>
          Compare against the baseline build in this `.kixbundle` file instead of the live cluster

      --detail <DETAIL>
          Detail level for `--output json` and `--output yaml` (text and Markdown output are unaffected)

          Possible values:
          - plain:     Resource names and changed field counts only
          - fields:    Include each changed field with its old and new values
          - manifests: Include the full old and new manifests for each changed, added, or removed resource
          
          [default: plain]

      --save <FILE>
          Write the comparison to a `.kixplan` file (same format as `kix plan --save`), regardless of `--detail` and `--output`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format (text, json, yaml, markdown). Default varies by command

          Possible values:
          - text
          - json
          - yaml
          - markdown: GitHub-flavored markdown suitable for PR comments
          
          [env: KIX_OUTPUT]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

      --context <CONTEXT>
          Kubeconfig context to use
          
          [env: KIX_CONTEXT]

      --flake <FLAKE>
          Flake reference to evaluate clusters from (default: current directory)
          
          [env: KIX_FLAKE]
          [default: .]

      --override-input <INPUT> <URL>
          Override a flake input's URL (repeatable: `--override-input INPUT URL`).
          
          Forwards `--override-input` to every `nix` subprocess. When this flag is not set, Kix reads space-separated `INPUT URL` pairs from `KIX_OVERRIDE_INPUT`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix plan

Preview a deploy as a server-side dry run

Runs the deploy pipeline with every write sent as a server-side dry run so the API server reports the exact field changes it would make and any writes it would reject (field ownership conflicts, admission webhooks, schema validation, or RBAC). Also reports stateful migrations, live drift that the deploy would revert, and orphaned resources that pruning would delete or keep. `kix deploy` prints this same plan before prompting, and `kix deploy --dry-run` runs this command.

Because dry-run writes require the same RBAC permissions as real writes, use `kix diff` when you only have read-only access. A dry run cannot verify whether Pods will start or pass readiness probes.

Exits 0 when the deploy would make no changes, 2 when changes would be made, and 3 when the deploy would be blocked by a rejected write or an unacknowledged stateful migration (see `--accept-migrations`).

Usage: kix plan [OPTIONS] <CLUSTER>

Arguments:
  <CLUSTER>
          Cluster name

Options:
      --current-activation
          Use the local activation cache instead of querying the cluster.
          
          Runs completely offline by comparing the new build against the locally cached activation. Does not inspect live cluster state, in-flight or failed deployments, or previously retained orphans, and performs no server-side dry run.

      --reconcile
          Re-apply all resources even if activation identity matches (heal drift)

      --prune
          Delete orphaned resources left from the previous activation or earlier deploys (`kix plan` dry-runs each deletion). Without this flag, orphans are kept and recorded on the new Activation for a later `--prune` or `kix gc`

      --prune-mode <MODE>
          Prune mode: default (prune after apply), no (skip pruning)

          Possible values:
          - default: Prune orphans after every apply has finished (default)
          - no:      Skip pruning entirely, defer to `kix gc`

      --force-conflicts
          Take ownership of fields currently owned by other field managers (SSA conflict override). Without this, conflicts surface as a 409 with the conflicting field paths and current owner. Useful when migrating resources from kubectl/helm to Kix for the first time

      --accept-migrations
          Acknowledge detected stateful migration pairs. Without this flag, a deploy that would remove and add stateful resources in the same logical slot is rejected (even with --yes) to prevent silent data loss, and `kix plan` exits 3. Kix does not copy data between them

      --bundle <FILE>
          Read the target build from a `.kixbundle` file instead of evaluating, verifying every store path against its content first

      --deployed-bundle <FILE>
          Bundle file for a previously deployed build (active, failed, or retained orphan source) to use when its store path is not present locally. Matched by the Activation record's identity hash; repeatable

      --save <FILE>
          Write the plan to a `.kixplan` file for `kix deploy --plan` or Kix Enterprise. Includes changed fields, the build's dependency graph, and full old and new manifests with Secret values redacted

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format (text, json, yaml, markdown). Default varies by command

          Possible values:
          - text
          - json
          - yaml
          - markdown: GitHub-flavored markdown suitable for PR comments
          
          [env: KIX_OUTPUT]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

      --context <CONTEXT>
          Kubeconfig context to use
          
          [env: KIX_CONTEXT]

      --flake <FLAKE>
          Flake reference to evaluate clusters from (default: current directory)
          
          [env: KIX_FLAKE]
          [default: .]

      --override-input <INPUT> <URL>
          Override a flake input's URL (repeatable: `--override-input INPUT URL`).
          
          Forwards `--override-input` to every `nix` subprocess. When this flag is not set, Kix reads space-separated `INPUT URL` pairs from `KIX_OVERRIDE_INPUT`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix check

Evaluate a cluster and report build-time checks and scorecard findings

Runs constructor validators, cross-resource checks, and error-severity scorecard rules; any failure marks the `eval` row as `fail`. The `scorecard` row summarizes findings by severity. To validate rendered manifests against Kubernetes schemas, run `nix flake check` (via the `schema-cluster-<name>` check). Exits 1 when any row reports `fail`.

Usage: kix check [OPTIONS] <CLUSTER>

Arguments:
  <CLUSTER>
          Cluster to validate

Options:
      --sarif
          Output SARIF 2.1.0 JSON (for GitHub Code Scanning integration)

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format (text, json, yaml, markdown). Default varies by command

          Possible values:
          - text
          - json
          - yaml
          - markdown: GitHub-flavored markdown suitable for PR comments
          
          [env: KIX_OUTPUT]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

      --flake <FLAKE>
          Flake reference to evaluate clusters from (default: current directory)
          
          [env: KIX_FLAKE]
          [default: .]

      --no-cache
          Skip the SQLite eval cache (always re-evaluate from Nix)
          
          [env: KIX_NO_CACHE]

      --override-input <INPUT> <URL>
          Override a flake input's URL (repeatable: `--override-input INPUT URL`).
          
          Forwards `--override-input` to every `nix` subprocess. When this flag is not set, Kix reads space-separated `INPUT URL` pairs from `KIX_OVERRIDE_INPUT`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix deploy

Wave-based deployment with confirmation

Usage: kix deploy [OPTIONS] <CLUSTER>

Arguments:
  <CLUSTER>
          Cluster name

Options:
      --current-activation
          Use the local activation cache instead of querying the cluster.
          
          Runs completely offline by comparing the new build against the locally cached activation. Does not inspect live cluster state, in-flight or failed deployments, or previously retained orphans, and performs no server-side dry run.

      --reconcile
          Re-apply all resources even if activation identity matches (heal drift)

      --prune
          Delete orphaned resources left from the previous activation or earlier deploys (`kix plan` dry-runs each deletion). Without this flag, orphans are kept and recorded on the new Activation for a later `--prune` or `kix gc`

      --prune-mode <MODE>
          Prune mode: default (prune after apply), no (skip pruning)

          Possible values:
          - default: Prune orphans after every apply has finished (default)
          - no:      Skip pruning entirely, defer to `kix gc`

      --force-conflicts
          Take ownership of fields currently owned by other field managers (SSA conflict override). Without this, conflicts surface as a 409 with the conflicting field paths and current owner. Useful when migrating resources from kubectl/helm to Kix for the first time

      --accept-migrations
          Acknowledge detected stateful migration pairs. Without this flag, a deploy that would remove and add stateful resources in the same logical slot is rejected (even with --yes) to prevent silent data loss, and `kix plan` exits 3. Kix does not copy data between them

      --bundle <FILE>
          Read the target build from a `.kixbundle` file instead of evaluating, verifying every store path against its content first

      --deployed-bundle <FILE>
          Bundle file for a previously deployed build (active, failed, or retained orphan source) to use when its store path is not present locally. Matched by the Activation record's identity hash; repeatable

  -y, --yes
          Skip confirmation prompt

      --dry-run
          Preview only: run `kix plan` with the same flags and make no changes. When combined with `--plan`, verify that the saved plan still matches and exit

      --plan <FILE>
          Deploy only if the current plan matches this `.kixplan` file from `kix plan --save`. Uses the planning flags recorded in the file and exits 4 without applying anything if the cluster state, builds, flags, refusals, reverted drift, or prune deletions have changed

      --on-error <ON_ERROR>
          Behavior on error: `stop` or `continue`. With `stop`, a deploy whose plan contains a refused write aborts before applying anything
          
          [default: stop]
          [possible values: stop, continue]

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Readiness timeout (e.g. "5m", "300s", or bare seconds)

      --operator
          Delegate deployment to the Kix operator instead of applying directly (requires the build in the cluster's Nix store; incompatible with `--bundle`)

      --record-annotation <KEY=VALUE>
          Add a `KEY=VALUE` annotation to the Activation record (repeatable). Keys under `kix.run/`, its subdomains, or retired Kix domains are reserved and rejected

      --tui
          Render a live inline graph-streaming dashboard. Falls back to line-oriented output on non-interactive terminals

      --group-by-ns
          Group the tree view by namespace instead of Kix package. Only affects rendering; execution order is unchanged

      --fold-height <ROWS>
          Cap the live viewport height in rows. Default: half the terminal height (clamped to [10, 40]). Rows beyond the cap fold into the scrollback history above

      --log-format <LOG_FORMAT>
          Output format for the deploy event stream. `pretty` (default on TTY) is the colored human stream; `line` is the nix-build-L idiom (one line per terminal verdict, no ANSI); `json` emits NDJSON, one event per line, suitable for CI/automation. Non-TTY stdout auto-falls-back to `line` when `pretty` is requested
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, line, json]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format (text, json, yaml, markdown). Default varies by command

          Possible values:
          - text
          - json
          - yaml
          - markdown: GitHub-flavored markdown suitable for PR comments
          
          [env: KIX_OUTPUT]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

      --context <CONTEXT>
          Kubeconfig context to use
          
          [env: KIX_CONTEXT]

      --flake <FLAKE>
          Flake reference to evaluate clusters from (default: current directory)
          
          [env: KIX_FLAKE]
          [default: .]

      --override-input <INPUT> <URL>
          Override a flake input's URL (repeatable: `--override-input INPUT URL`).
          
          Forwards `--override-input` to every `nix` subprocess. When this flag is not set, Kix reads space-separated `INPUT URL` pairs from `KIX_OVERRIDE_INPUT`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

