          Print help

## kix lib update

Move this project's Kix library to the version of the installed Kix

Locks the project's Kix library input (github:kix-run/kix) to the tag `v<version>` of the installed Kix and writes flake.lock. It does not change flake.nix. When flake.nix pins another release, or the input is a local path or a fork, it says what to change by hand.

Usage: kix lib update [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --dry-run
          Show what would change without writing flake.lock

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

      --flake <FLAKE>
          Flake reference to evaluate clusters from (default: current directory)
          
          [env: KIX_FLAKE]
          [default: .]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix self

Install, list, and remove versions of Kix

Versions live under ~/.local/share/kix/<version>. A project names the version it needs with `kix = "0.8"` in kix.toml, and Kix runs that version for the project, installing it first when needed.

Usage: kix self [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  install  Install a version of Kix
  list     List the installed versions, newest first
  prune    Remove all but the newest installed versions
  update   Install the newest release and point ~/.local/bin/kix at it
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix self install

Install a version of Kix

Takes a minor such as 0.8, which installs its newest patch, or an exact version such as 0.8.3. Kix checks the release's signature and SHA-256 before it unpacks anything.

Usage: kix self install [OPTIONS] <VERSION>

Arguments:
  <VERSION>
          The version to install, such as 0.8 or 0.8.3

Options:
  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix self list

List the installed versions, newest first

Marks the running version with `*` and the one `kix` on PATH runs.

Usage: kix self list [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix self prune

Remove all but the newest installed versions

Always keeps the running version and the one `kix` on PATH runs.

Usage: kix self prune [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --keep <KEEP>
          How many of the newest versions to keep
          
          [default: 2]

      --dry-run
          Show what would be removed without removing it

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

## kix self update

Install the newest release and point ~/.local/bin/kix at it

Works when the install script installed this Kix. Otherwise it says how to upgrade Kix the way it was installed.

Usage: kix self update [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -v, --verbose...
          Increase verbosity (-v, -vv, etc.)
          
          [env: KIX_VERBOSE]

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output
          
          [env: KIX_QUIET]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output (also respects NO_COLOR env per no-color.org spec)

